1
00:00:00,710 --> 00:00:01,830
It's three in the morning,

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:04,280
and a customer just messaged your business.

3
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:05,420
Who answers?

4
00:00:05,820 --> 00:00:08,400
If the answer is "nobody until nine," that

5
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:11,840
customer is probably already talking to someone else.

6
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:13,050
In the next few minutes,

7
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:16,100
I'll show you exactly how an AI receptionist

8
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:18,620
answers that message, and what separates a good

9
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:20,580
one from an annoying one.

10
00:00:21,130 --> 00:00:23,870
Most small businesses are open about forty hours

11
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:24,540
a week.

12
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:27,740
Customers message all one hundred and sixty-eight.

13
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:31,020
Evenings, weekends, other time zones.

14
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:32,900
And every channel is different:

15
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:35,820
your website, WhatsApp, the phone.

16
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:38,280
An AI receptionist sits on all of them

17
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:40,900
at once, and never goes off shift.

18
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:43,380
Part one: the channels.

19
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:46,380
This is wherever your customers already are:

20
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:48,480
a chat window on your website,

21
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,030
a voice button in the browser,

22
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:51,380
your WhatsApp number.

23
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:53,520
The trick is that all of them feed

24
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:56,000
one single agent, so the answer is the

25
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:58,200
same wherever the customer asks.

26
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,670
Part two: the brain.

27
00:01:00,690 --> 00:01:02,490
That's a large language model,

28
00:01:02,510 --> 00:01:04,920
the same kind of AI behind the chat

29
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:06,290
assistants you've already used.

30
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:08,720
It's great at understanding messy,

31
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:11,430
real human messages, typos and all.

32
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:12,570
But on its own,

33
00:01:12,590 --> 00:01:14,730
it knows nothing about your business.

34
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:16,290
Ask it your opening hours,

35
00:01:16,310 --> 00:01:18,090
and it'll happily guess.

36
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:20,620
Which is why part three matters most:

37
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:22,280
the knowledge base.

38
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:24,460
You give the agent your own information:

39
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:28,100
services, prices, hours, policies.

40
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:29,760
When a question comes in,

41
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:32,820
the system first finds the few passages that

42
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:35,070
actually answer it, and hands only those to

43
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:36,000
the brain.

44
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:39,730
Engineers call this retrieval-augmented generation,

45
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:40,560
R-A-G.

46
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:42,510
In plain words: the AI looks it up

47
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:44,260
before it speaks.

48
00:01:44,410 --> 00:01:47,230
That's the difference between "I think we're open

49
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:49,120
Sundays" and "We're open Sundays,

50
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:50,475
ten to four."

51
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:52,970
Part four: actions.

52
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:54,830
Answering questions is nice,

53
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:56,850
but the goal is the lead.

54
00:01:57,050 --> 00:01:59,450
A good receptionist collects the customer's name,

55
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:01,940
what they need, and how to reach them,

56
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:04,430
and sends it straight to the owner's inbox.

57
00:02:04,710 --> 00:02:06,680
You wake up to booked conversations,

58
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:08,310
not missed ones.

59
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:11,800
Now, we've all met a bad chatbot.

60
00:02:11,820 --> 00:02:13,290
So here's the checklist.

61
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:16,410
One: it answers only from your information,

62
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:18,810
and says "I don't know" instead of guessing.

63
00:02:18,830 --> 00:02:20,990
Two: it hands off to a human for

64
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:23,130
anything it shouldn't decide.

65
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:25,950
Three: it captures the lead every time,

66
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:28,410
not just the conversation.

67
00:02:28,830 --> 00:02:29,560
Get those three right,

68
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:32,620
and customers barely notice they're talking to an

69
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,370
AI.

70
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:36,550
This is exactly how we built ClientFalcon Voice

71
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:39,550
at iAgentice: voice and chat assistants trained on

72
00:02:39,570 --> 00:02:41,770
a business's own information,

73
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:45,140
answering website visitors and WhatsApp messages around the

74
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:47,630
clock, and sending every lead to the owner's

75
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:48,450
inbox.

76
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:50,150
If you want one for your business,

77
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:52,270
or want us to build a custom agent,

78
00:02:52,330 --> 00:02:53,570
the link's in the description.

79
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:55,210
And if you'd like to see the build

80
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:56,610
itself, step by step,

81
00:02:56,630 --> 00:02:57,530
subscribe.

82
00:02:57,810 --> 00:02:59,290
That's the next video.
